With that, let's start with a introductory question for someone who's never worked with ERP.

30
00:01:58,870 --> 00:02:03,749
Subjit, how would you explain what it is and why it's central to running a business?

31
00:02:03,759 --> 00:02:05,910
Enterprise resource planning system.

32
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:08,070
That's the full form of ERP.

33
00:02:08,070 --> 00:02:13,510
So we can see like in the full form also enterprise is there.

34
00:02:13,510 --> 00:02:19,190
So this is a total software solution for enterprise to run the business.

35
00:02:19,190 --> 00:02:27,510
ERP will integrate from finance to procurement to production to inventory to sales to logistics to shipping.

36
00:02:27,510 --> 00:02:30,470
and each and every business system.

37
00:02:30,470 --> 00:02:33,190
So without ERP all these processes will be running in silos.

38
00:02:33,190 --> 00:02:37,030
So that will be creating inefficiency that will be creating lot of lagging.

39
00:02:37,030 --> 00:02:41,350
and there will not be visibility of the business processes at any level.

40
00:02:41,350 --> 00:02:49,351
That's the main reason if you see all the Fortune 500 companies are running in one kind of. ERPs.

41
00:02:49,351 --> 00:02:52,070
or some of the companies will be running in multiple ERPs.

42
00:02:52,070 --> 00:03:00,630
I can tell the ERP vendors who are really leaders in ERP industry like SAP, Oracle, Microsoft Dynamics.

43
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:05,589
and now from the example I can tell some kind of you know idea.

44
00:03:05,589 --> 00:03:11,270
like who have never worked with ERP or who doesn't know what is ERP.

45
00:03:11,270 --> 00:03:15,110
So let's say without a total software solution like ERP sales department got the sales order.

46
00:03:15,110 --> 00:03:17,350
So it will be in an excel.

47
00:03:17,350 --> 00:03:24,071
and then sales department will be sending those sales order requirement to production department in a mail. or excel.

48
00:03:24,071 --> 00:03:29,830
and then from that production department they will be requesting to the warehouse for having the components for.

49
00:03:29,830 --> 00:03:31,750
manufacturing the finish groups.

50
00:03:31,750 --> 00:03:36,149
So now we can see here there are lot of manual processes there are lot of manual interaction.

51
00:03:36,149 --> 00:03:38,229
with different departments.

52
00:03:38,229 --> 00:03:40,470
So there is a lot of data lagging.

53
00:03:40,470 --> 00:03:44,869
and inefficiencies it can happen but with ERP it will never happen.

54
00:03:44,869 --> 00:03:48,550
because it will be a total same software platform.

55
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:52,149
and you can say ERP is the nervous system of an organization to run the business.

56
00:03:52,159 --> 00:04:02,470
Thanks with that background can you walk us through a flow how ERP shows up in day-to-day operations?

57
00:04:02,470 --> 00:04:05,110
You mentioned it joins multiple departments together.

58
00:04:05,110 --> 00:04:13,429
So maybe could you talk about how a customer order turns into procurement, production, shipping and finally cash.

59
00:04:13,439 --> 00:04:15,429
Yeah.

60
00:04:15,429 --> 00:04:17,670
So if we are talking about the basic ERP flows,

61
00:04:17,670 --> 00:04:20,550
I would like to mention three basic ERP flows.

62
00:04:20,550 --> 00:04:23,749
Order to cash, plan to produce and procure to pay.

63
00:04:23,749 --> 00:04:31,671
Now with order to cache, ERP manages those customer orders like a sales order through delivery to customer. invoicing.

64
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:37,270
And then for plan to produce process it will be a demand from sales order.

65
00:04:37,270 --> 00:04:41,189
So there will be production order to manufacture the finished goods.

66
00:04:41,189 --> 00:04:48,790
And then with procure to pay process ERP will create purchase order to external vendors.

67
00:04:48,790 --> 00:04:51,270
for the components which are required to manufacture the product.

68
00:04:51,270 --> 00:04:56,070
So I can give one example from electronics manufacturing industry.

69
00:04:56,070 --> 00:05:05,430
where the products is like laptop, mobile, cloud computing infrastructure like racks, servers, then industrial drones.

70
00:05:05,430 --> 00:05:11,111
and you name any kind of you know electronics component it will be a product for electronics manufacturing. industry.

71
00:05:11,111 --> 00:05:16,230
So now let's take one example for one familiar product like a laptop.

72
00:05:16,230 --> 00:05:18,310
So we all are ordering laptop.

73
00:05:18,310 --> 00:05:24,230
So whenever we are ordering laptop we are basically selecting a different specification right.

74
00:05:24,230 --> 00:05:29,111
like we are selecting what will be the microprocessor speed what will be the chassis what will be. the RAM.

75
00:05:29,111 --> 00:05:35,590
so from ERP point of view these are all components.

76
00:05:35,590 --> 00:05:37,990
and these components are in a bill of material.

77
00:05:37,990 --> 00:05:43,270
so that's the way we are tracking all the components for manufacturing the laptop.

78
00:05:43,270 --> 00:05:46,710
now let's say from manufacturing the laptop.

79
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:53,510
if sales department got a sales order for manufacturing let's say 100 numbers of laptops.

80
00:05:53,510 --> 00:05:59,749
So for 100 numbers of laptops there will be components all will be needed for procuring.

81
00:05:59,749 --> 00:06:02,790
if those components will not be in the company stock.

82
00:06:02,790 --> 00:06:08,629
So with procurement like a external purchase order to vendors all the components will be coming to company.

83
00:06:08,639 --> 00:06:11,350
stock.

84
00:06:11,350 --> 00:06:17,750
then only all these manufacturing orders will be fulfilled with the components to manufacture the 100 numbers of. laptops.

85
00:06:17,750 --> 00:06:22,629
So after having the 100 numbers of laptops that will be delivered to customer.

86
00:06:22,629 --> 00:06:28,790
Now if I am just mapping all these processes in these three basic ERP processes.

87
00:06:28,790 --> 00:06:33,749
So order to cash is the sales order and delivery of the 100 numbers of laptops.

88
00:06:33,749 --> 00:06:38,790
Plan to produce will be the manufacturing order which will be internal to the company.

89
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:43,510
where this 100 numbers of laptops will be manufactured.

90
00:06:43,510 --> 00:06:47,909
and then procured to pay cycle will be the external purchase order.

91
00:06:47,909 --> 00:06:50,710
that went to the vendors for supplying all the components.

92
00:06:50,710 --> 00:06:56,550
So in this way you can have all these business processes mapped in the three basic ERP process.

93
00:06:56,560 --> 00:06:58,391
Thank you. That's very helpful.

94
00:06:58,391 --> 00:07:04,070
Now before we dive in further, can you just take a step back.

95
00:07:04,070 --> 00:07:06,390
and describe how ERP has evolved over time?

96
00:07:06,390 --> 00:07:10,390
So in the beginning there were traditional onremise systems.

97
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:13,270
then there was the SAS evolution to SAS.

98
00:07:13,270 --> 00:07:17,189
and how that set the stage for ML geni and now agent based systems.

99
00:07:17,199 --> 00:07:21,589
Yeah.

100
00:07:21,589 --> 00:07:23,510
So ERP is a old software concept.

101
00:07:23,510 --> 00:07:26,469
So it's it's nothing new, right?

102
00:07:26,469 --> 00:07:29,909
So I would say ERP has begun maybe 1990s.

103
00:07:29,909 --> 00:07:33,510
and then I started working on ERP in onremise software.

104
00:07:33,510 --> 00:07:40,870
So it was around 2005 and then it really evolves from there.

105
00:07:40,870 --> 00:07:43,909
So around 2010 it went to SAS ERP delivery model.

106
00:07:43,909 --> 00:07:51,350
where all the infrastructures like all the servers application software data and managed by the ERP vendor.

107
00:07:51,350 --> 00:07:58,950
So company doesn't have to take any kind of initiative for installation maintenance of the ERP software.

108
00:07:58,950 --> 00:08:01,029
and that SAS ERP will be available over the internet to the user.

109
00:08:01,039 --> 00:08:07,749
Now from SAS ERP like I would say 2 3 years back we started with you know AI.

110
00:08:07,759 --> 00:08:09,749
capability.

111
00:08:09,749 --> 00:08:11,830
So that time we are talking about ML model.

112
00:08:11,830 --> 00:08:18,790
that is the machine learning algorithms where we can have all the patterns with the big ERP data.

113
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:26,710
From there ML model we'll be having predictive analytics for any kind of business function.

114
00:08:26,710 --> 00:08:29,749
like anomaly detection like warehouse efficiency improvement.

115
00:08:29,759 --> 00:08:32,870
and now we are talking about JNI agents.

116
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:37,750
So if we are having a chatbot we are always familiar with the chatbots.

117
00:08:37,750 --> 00:08:39,670
Now if we are thinking about the chatbot.

118
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:45,910
that will be directing us how to operate the business process.

119
00:08:45,910 --> 00:08:48,630
that's where the ji agents have come.

120
00:08:48,630 --> 00:08:51,750
like you can just write in a chat GPT like thing.

121
00:08:51,750 --> 00:08:57,590
and then that ji agent will direct you for the correct insight from the ERP.

122
00:08:57,590 --> 00:08:59,509
So this is the way it really evaluated.

123
00:08:59,519 --> 00:09:07,829
Do you have a real example of what happens when an ERP system goes down or went down?

124
00:09:07,829 --> 00:09:10,230
What broke first in the business and did it escalate?

125
00:09:10,230 --> 00:09:12,949
Just walk us through what the chaos might have ensured.

126
00:09:12,959 --> 00:09:17,030
Yeah, it will be a complete chaos obviously.

127
00:09:17,030 --> 00:09:23,110
because as I said earlier ERP system is the nervous system of a business.

128
00:09:23,110 --> 00:09:25,509
So if ERP system goes down then production order will be halted.

129
00:09:25,519 --> 00:09:32,150
Procurement will not be happening because buyer will not be sending the purchase order to vendors.

130
00:09:32,150 --> 00:09:34,070
Shipment will be delayed.

131
00:09:34,070 --> 00:09:35,590
So order will not be fulfilled.

132
00:09:35,590 --> 00:09:38,949
So there will be lot of revenue loss.

133
00:09:38,949 --> 00:09:42,870
So to have this kind of downtime because ERP is a software right.

134
00:09:42,870 --> 00:09:45,350
So for any kind of software this update maintenance is required.

135
00:09:45,350 --> 00:09:51,110
So to avoid this kind of disruption there are planned downtime that is required always.

136
00:09:51,120 --> 00:09:56,790
for having the update in the ERP for latest kind of uh software update.

137
00:09:56,790 --> 00:10:02,230
and that time there will be different strategy that organization needs to take.

138
00:10:02,240 --> 00:10:06,070
like there will be a lot of you know offline entries like the manual entries.

139
00:10:06,080 --> 00:10:07,910
that will be done.

140
00:10:07,910 --> 00:10:11,030
and after that it will be entered in the ERP.

141
00:10:11,030 --> 00:10:13,750
So in that way those kind of downtime can be managed.

142
00:10:13,760 --> 00:10:15,910
Got it?

143
00:10:15,910 --> 00:10:21,110
Now that we've covered the basics of ERP, let's talk about implementation.

144
00:10:21,110 --> 00:10:26,791
What does an ERP implementation actually look like from start to go live? Yeah.

145
00:10:26,791 --> 00:10:30,230
So ERP implementation is a structured process.

146
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:34,949
I would say it will be six to seven stages.

147
00:10:34,949 --> 00:10:37,030
So it will be starting with the project preparation.

148
00:10:37,030 --> 00:10:42,949
where the business problems will be finalized and the project will be finalized.

149
00:10:42,949 --> 00:10:47,110
Project manager and project team will be assigned.

150
00:10:47,110 --> 00:10:53,030
and then there will be business blueprint phase where that business problem will be discussed with business team.

151
00:10:53,030 --> 00:10:55,829
to understand what will be the changes in the ERP system.

152
00:10:55,829 --> 00:10:58,230
and then as is and 2B documents will be prepared.

153
00:10:58,230 --> 00:11:03,509
and then in the next phase it will be system design.

154
00:11:03,509 --> 00:11:09,910
and realization phase where project team will be changing in the ERP system.

155
00:11:09,910 --> 00:11:12,630
So it can be a enhancement for the existing functionality.

156
00:11:12,630 --> 00:11:19,430
or it can be a new transaction that is needed for users.

157
00:11:19,430 --> 00:11:21,910
or it can be the integration with the third party systems.

158
00:11:21,910 --> 00:11:26,470
like it can be MES system that needs to be integrated with the ERP.

159
00:11:26,470 --> 00:11:28,949
that is the manufacturing execution system.

160
00:11:28,949 --> 00:11:30,630
which is very common in electronics manufacturing industry.

161
00:11:30,640 --> 00:11:39,269
because all the production operations in electronics manufacturing needs to be tracked at very granular level.

162
00:11:39,269 --> 00:11:41,509
So, MEES system will be integrated.

163
00:11:41,509 --> 00:11:43,670
For that, we need to develop different framework.

164
00:11:43,670 --> 00:11:51,270
and then we have to also do different framework for businessto business communication with the different customers. and vendors.

165
00:11:51,270 --> 00:11:56,470
like we have to send maybe inventory data to the customers.

166
00:11:56,470 --> 00:12:02,710
and they want to have the visibility of company stock how the raw materials are coming.

167
00:12:02,710 --> 00:12:05,190
according to that they will be placing the orders.

168
00:12:05,190 --> 00:12:08,470
So having said this will be the phase.

169
00:12:08,470 --> 00:12:14,069
where ERP team will be really doing the changes in the system.

170
00:12:14,069 --> 00:12:19,350
and then there will be the next stage where all these changes will be tested.

171
00:12:19,350 --> 00:12:21,750
It can be testing within ERP system.

172
00:12:21,750 --> 00:12:24,790
It can be testing with the MES system.

173
00:12:24,790 --> 00:12:26,710
For MES system there will be different team.

174
00:12:26,710 --> 00:12:31,670
It can be the testing with the vendor or customer team.

175
00:12:31,670 --> 00:12:39,350
because vendor and customer also needs to test their own functionality with this B2B communication.

176
00:12:39,350 --> 00:12:43,990
So after all this testing successful there will be final preparation.

177
00:12:43,990 --> 00:12:45,829
and go live phase where there will be user training.

178
00:12:45,829 --> 00:12:50,069
because user needs to know what will be the new functionalities.

179
00:12:50,069 --> 00:12:54,470
Already some of the users like super users they will be testing those functionalities.

180
00:12:54,470 --> 00:13:02,069
and after their testing certification only all these new changes will be going to the production.

181
00:13:02,069 --> 00:13:07,910
So for all other users there should be training to operate the new functionalities.

182
00:13:07,910 --> 00:13:12,629
and then for new site implementation there will be data migration.

183
00:13:12,629 --> 00:13:17,430
like there may be some of the new inventory that needs to be updated in the new site.

184
00:13:17,430 --> 00:13:21,190
and there will be different new beans that needs to be created.

185
00:13:21,190 --> 00:13:23,750
So those are some kind of example for the data migration.

186
00:13:23,750 --> 00:13:31,990
So after all this preparation training data migration strategy program then there will be go live.

187
00:13:31,990 --> 00:13:33,990
So in this phase it can be technical go live.

188
00:13:33,990 --> 00:13:36,230
and after that it can be business gloive.

189
00:13:36,230 --> 00:13:41,509
Sometime what will happen the bigger changes which can affect the whole ERP system globally.

190
00:13:41,519 --> 00:13:47,670
Those changes will be sent at the system downtime.

191
00:13:47,670 --> 00:13:49,350
So the business go live will not be happening immediately.

192
00:13:49,350 --> 00:13:53,910
that business go live will be happening after the technical go live.

193
00:13:53,910 --> 00:13:56,230
So after go live obviously we need to support the system.

194
00:13:56,240 --> 00:13:58,230
if there will be any kind of issues.

195
00:13:58,240 --> 00:14:06,069
usually it will be saying as hyperare support like we have to resolve that issues very fast.

196
00:14:06,069 --> 00:14:09,590
So there will be very strict SLA for that.

197
00:14:09,590 --> 00:14:14,629
So after this hyperare support it will be handed over to support team.

198
00:14:14,629 --> 00:14:16,870
and that will be the end of that project.

199
00:14:16,870 --> 00:14:20,150
So this is a pretty good flow of the ERP implementation methodology.

200
00:14:20,160 --> 00:14:22,470
So you did call out integrations.

201
00:14:22,470 --> 00:14:24,310
You give an example of that.

202
00:14:24,310 --> 00:14:29,990
You talked about data migration and then you also said in some cases there are enhancements needed.

203
00:14:29,990 --> 00:14:31,829
Do you have an example of an enhancement?

204
00:14:31,839 --> 00:14:35,110
Yeah, sure.

205
00:14:35,110 --> 00:14:38,310
So let's say we need to have one interface that needs to build.

206
00:14:38,310 --> 00:14:44,870
so that we can send the inventory data to a customer.

207
00:14:44,870 --> 00:14:47,910
and we do not have this B2B communication with that customer.

208
00:14:47,910 --> 00:14:52,069
So we have to do the changes in ERP system.

209
00:14:52,069 --> 00:14:57,670
and we have to do the testing with the file whatever we are sending.

210
00:14:57,670 --> 00:15:03,189
whether that file is getting received by the customer or not.

211
00:15:03,189 --> 00:15:05,670
So this is a kind of enhancement that we have to do in our system.

212
00:15:05,670 --> 00:15:10,470
as well as we have to do integration testing between customer system.

213
00:15:10,470 --> 00:15:14,310
So this is an example like if we are building some kind of you know new interface.

214
00:15:14,320 --> 00:15:18,629
Could you maybe share an example of an implementation.

215
00:15:18,629 --> 00:15:21,670
that went well and talk about what made it succeed?

216
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:28,069
So I would like to share one of the biggest implementation.

217
00:15:28,069 --> 00:15:31,350
that I was a part of and that went very well.

218
00:15:31,350 --> 00:15:37,910
So it was SAP S4ANA implementation 3 to four years back.

219
00:15:37,910 --> 00:15:40,710
So as I said SAP is one of the biggest ERP vendor.

220
00:15:40,710 --> 00:15:44,150
and their latest technology is for HANA.

221
00:15:44,150 --> 00:15:47,590
So HANA is inmemory platform.

222
00:15:47,590 --> 00:15:50,790
So the transactions of the ERP will be very fast.

223
00:15:50,790 --> 00:15:56,870
So that's the main benefit by going to SAP S4 HANA.

224
00:15:56,870 --> 00:15:58,949
So earlier we are having onremise system as I said earlier.

225
00:15:58,949 --> 00:16:01,990
So it's a kind of evaluation.

226
00:16:01,990 --> 00:16:06,150
So we went to cloud and to SAP S4 HANA cloud.

227
00:16:06,150 --> 00:16:10,870
So that time 3 to 4 years ago there is no program available from SAP like today it.

228
00:16:10,870 --> 00:16:13,030
is available SAP rise.

229
00:16:13,030 --> 00:16:17,189
where you can go to private cloud that time it was not there.

230
00:16:17,189 --> 00:16:23,990
So we have to basically go through our own methodology own framework own testing strategy own role strategy.

231
00:16:23,990 --> 00:16:28,069
role means like the authorization what authorization we'll be having for the user.

